  "final_text": "All four steps are done in Kibana 9.5.5, and the rule reads back as enabled.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' (`kibana_sample_data_flights`, id `d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`) has 35 fields. The main ones are `timestamp` (date), `Carrier`, `Cancelled` (boolean), `FlightDelay`, `DistanceKilometers`, `Origin`/`Dest`, and `AvgTicketPrice`. The time field is `timestamp`.\n\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-ae86c9d4 Flights' (id `8253d163-e62b-4c57-b393-bc464e1097f6`) has exactly two panels, both on the flights data view:\n   - a line chart of flight count over a date histogram on `timestamp`\n   - a bar chart of flight count by `Carrier`\n\n   The dashboard time range is my choice: last 7 days.\n\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-ae86c9d4 Cancelled flights' (id `51a1a7a1-d5b3-4a21-842b-f6337e797a1c`) is an Elasticsearch query rule (`.es-query`) on the flights data view. It counts documents matching `Cancelled : true` over a 5-minute window and fires when the count is greater than 10. It runs every 1m. The rule has no actions and uses the `alerts` consumer. It was created enabled, so no enable call was needed.\n\n4. **Rename and read-back:** The carrier panel is now titled 'Flights by carrier'. The dashboard still has two panels. Reading the rule back shows `enabled: True`, with the schedule at 1m and the last run succeeded.\n\nThe carrier chart shows Kibana's default top 5 carriers, not every carrier. Say if you want all of them.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
